Objective:Implement Decision Tree and Linear SVM classifiers from scratch, compare them with Scikit-learn, visualize decision boundaries, and explore 
nonlinear SVM kernels.

Project Overview
This project explores two fundamental machine learning classification algorithms: Decision Trees and Support Vector Machines (SVMs).
Both algorithms are implemented from scratch using NumPy to understand their underlying mathematical principles.
Their performance is compared with Scikit-learn implementations, while nonlinear SVM kernels are explored using a synthetic dataset.
The project concludes with a comprehensive performance evaluation using the original Banknote Authentication dataset.

Features used:
- Variance — Variance of the wavelet-transformed image.
- Skewness — Skewness of the wavelet-transformed image.
- Curtosis — Curtosis of the wavelet-transformed image.
- Entropy — Entropy of the image.

Target: Class label indicating authentic or forged banknotes.


Objectives:
- Implement Gini impurity and entropy for Decision Tree splitting.
- Develop a recursive Decision Tree classifier from scratch.
- Implement a soft-margin Linear SVM using hinge loss and gradient descent.
- Visualize decision boundaries, tree structures, and SVM margins.
- Investigate the effects of tree depth, SVM regularization, learning rate, and iterations.
- Explore Linear, Polynomial, and RBF kernels on nonlinear data.
- Compare models using classification metrics and execution times.


Project Workflow
Part A — Decision Tree from Scratch
- Calculate Gini impurity and entropy.
- Identify optimal feature-threshold splits.
- Construct the tree recursively using stopping conditions.
- Implement class prediction.
- Visualize decision boundaries and learned tree structures.
- Compare criteria and maximum-depth settings against Scikit-learn.

Part B — Linear SVM from Scratch
- Standardize features using training-set statistics.
- Convert binary labels to −1 and +1.
- Implement a soft-margin SVM using hinge loss and gradient descent.
- Plot the separating hyperplane, margins, and margin-violating samples.
- Investigate the effects of regularization parameter `C`, learning rate, and iterations.

Part C — Nonlinear SVM Kernels
- Generate a nonlinear dataset using `make_moons`.
- Train SVM classifiers with Linear, Polynomial, and RBF kernels.
- Visualize and compare their decision boundaries.
- Analyze how nonlinear kernels capture complex patterns.

Part D — Final Performance Comparison
Train and evaluate the manual and Scikit-learn Decision Trees, manual and Scikit-learn Linear SVMs, and an RBF SVM using all four Banknote dataset features.


Visualizations:
The project includes:
- Manual Decision Tree decision boundary.
- Scikit-learn Decision Tree decision boundary.
- Learned tree structure and split rules.
- Manual Linear SVM separating hyperplane and margins.
- Scikit-learn Linear SVM decision boundary.
- Linear, Polynomial, and RBF kernel decision boundaries.
- Model performance comparison.

Key Learnings:
- Decision Trees provide interpretable classification rules but can overfit when their complexity is not controlled.
- SVMs rely on margin maximization and generally benefit from feature scaling.
- Regularization influences the balance between training errors and model complexity.
- Nonlinear kernels enable SVMs to learn curved decision boundaries.
- From-scratch implementations help explain algorithmic behavior, while Scikit-learn provides optimized benchmark implementations.
- Accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and execution time offer complementary perspectives on model performance.
